Now let me understand the vulnerability. The issue is about `jv_string_empty` not properly null-terminating the data. Let me look at `jvp_string_empty_new`:

```c
static jv jvp_string_empty_new(uint32_t length) {
  jvp_string* s = jvp_string_alloc(length);
  s->length_hashed = 0;
  memset(s->data, 0, length);
  jv r = {JVP_FLAGS_STRING, 0, 0, 0, {&s->refcnt}};
  return r;
}
```

`jvp_string_alloc(length)` allocates `sizeof(jvp_string) + length + 1` bytes. So `s->data` has `length + 1` bytes available. `memset(s->data, 0, length)` zeroes `length` bytes. The `+1` byte (the null terminator position at `s->data[length]`) is NOT zeroed.

Wait, but `jv_mem_alloc` - does it zero memory? Let me check. Actually `jv_mem_alloc` is likely just `malloc`. So the byte at `s->data[length]` is uninitialized.

Compare with `jvp_string_new`:
```c
static jv jvp_string_new(const char* data, uint32_t length) {
  jvp_string* s = jvp_string_alloc(length);
  s->length_hashed = length << 1;
  if (data != NULL)
    memcpy(s->data, data, length);
  s->data[length] = 0;  // <-- null terminator set
  ...
}
```

So `jvp_string_new` sets `s->data[length] = 0`, but `jvp_string_empty_new` does NOT set `s->data[length] = 0`. It only does `memset(s->data, 0, length)` which zeroes bytes 0..length-1, but NOT byte at index `length`.

So when `length == 0` (empty string), `memset(s->data, 0, 0)` does nothing, and `s->data[0]` is uninitialized. The string data is not null-terminated.

